  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user: \"Explain how a relational database executes a SQL query, from parsing and planning through indexing, joins, transactions, and returning rows. Write a detailed technical tutorial with concrete examples and tradeoffs, including common performance pitfalls. Use complete sentences and avoid repeating yourself.\"\n\nNeed produce detailed technical tutorial. Need final only. Need be thorough, complete sentences, avoid repetition. Need cover parsing, planning, indexing, joins, transactions, returning rows. Concrete examples and tradeoffs, common performance pitfalls.\n\nNeed structure. Could use headings. Need ensure complete sentences. Avoid repeating. Let's plan.\n\nTitle: How a relational database executes a SQL query.\n\nSections:\n1. High-level pipeline: client sends SQL, server parses, validates, optimizes, executes, returns.\n2. Parsing and validation: lexical analysis, syntax tree, semantic analysis, name resolution, type checking, privilege checks. Example SELECT with ambiguous column, missing table. Tradeoffs: strict vs permissive, prepared statements.\n3. Query rewriting and normalization: view expansion, common subexpression, predicate pushdown, constant folding. Example WHERE 1=1, IN list.\n4. Optimization/planning: logical plan, physical plan, cost model, statistics, cardinality estimates, access paths. Explain plan. Example choose index vs seq scan. Tradeoffs: cost model inaccuracies, optimizer hints, parameterized queries, plan caching.\n5. Indexes: B-tree, hash, GIN/GiST, covering, composite, partial, expression. How used: point lookup, range, sort, join, aggregate. Example orders table. Tradeoffs: write amplification, storage, maintenance, selectivity, index-only scans, covering indexes. Pitfalls: leading wildcard, function on column, implicit type conversion, low selectivity, over-indexing, index fragmentation, stale stats.\n6. Execution engine: Volcano/pull model, operators: scan, filter, join, aggregate, sort, limit. Example plan.\n7. Joins: nested loop, hash join, merge join, index join. Example orders/customers. Tradeoffs: memory, sorting, cardinality, skew. Pitfalls: cross joins, fan-out, non-equi joins, join order, missing indexes, large hash tables, data skew.\n8. Transactions and concurrency: ACID, isolation levels, MVCC, locks, snapshots, WAL, commit/rollback. How query sees data. Example read committed vs repeatable read",
